# SYNOPSIS

```text
userboot.next=bin/launcher launcher.until=board-test
```

# DESCRIPTION

board-test is the board image's last service: the image a board is booted with to see that it is stable.
The image holds every service, the console and its shell among them, with fs(4) on an NVMe disk's `taisce` partition, the first of `/dev/sdN0` and `/dev/sdN1` to have one (on the Orange Pi 6 Plus its second disk; its first is FreeBSD's), a volume made there the first time, but not the test programs or the launcher's own test.
Its cpu image, `bootfs-board-cpu.img`, is the same with a cpu server (cpu(8)) for this terminal, running for good: the board's end of the bring-up link (vx-cpu(1), vx-remote(1)'s `--image board-cpu`).
The launcher runs until board-test ends and exits with its code (launcher(8)); the board image's command line then reboots, so a board whose firmware boots the image once returns to its own system.

It waits up to 30 seconds for devmgr(8) to bind its drivers, then checks that every device devmgr bound has its driver running, a PCI function's with its DMA on an IOMMU (not `passthrough` or `none`; an ACPI device that firmware puts behind no IOMMU, as the Orange Pi 6 Plus's USB controllers, may be `passthrough`), that every IOMMU devmgr names is `ok`, and that each disk at `/dev/sd`* answers its `ctl` with a `geometry` line, which needs the driver to have read the disk (its partition table) through its DMA.
It writes a MiB to `/data/board-test`, removing the last run's first and waiting up to 10 seconds for fs(4) to serve the volume, and checks that it reads back the same.
It checks that each USB controller's xhci(3) has looked at the devices on its bus (`bus ready`); in QEMU, whose USB keyboards and mice are vendor `0627`'s, it then plugs a keyboard into the hub there (`qemu: device_add`), checks that xhci finds the hub, the mouse on it and the keyboard, types a key on it (`qemu: sendkey a`), and checks that xhci gave kbdfs(4) its press and release.
It checks that the SCMI agent, scmi(3), where devmgr runs one, answers on each channel it has, and that each GPU's mali(3) powered the GPU up and read its GPU_ID (`probed`), waiting up to 10 seconds.
Then it waits up to 90 seconds from its start for `/net/ether0`'s link to come up and for ip(4) to take an IPv4 address on it, by DHCP or as configured, which needs frames to have gone both ways.
It prints a line for each check, `board-test: ok:` or `board-test: FAILED:` and what it checks, each bound device's line of devmgr's status, each IOMMU, each disk's `ctl`, the interface's status, and at the end each driver's own `status`; its last line is `board-test: passed` or `board-test: FAILED, <n> checks`.
It exits 0 if every check passed.

